WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.150
(mysterious music)

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:04.890
- [Presenter] Computer
chips probably make you

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.157
think of this.

00:00:06.157 --> 00:00:07.950
("Intel theme music")

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:10.170
Since it was founded in 1968,

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.510
Intel has dominated the chip market.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.400
- [Voiceover] This symbol outside

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.470
means you have the standard inside.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:18.210
- [Presenter] It was the
world's largest chip maker

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.332
by revenue for 25 years

00:00:20.332 --> 00:00:23.910
and it's still America's
largest chip company by sales

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.290
- [Voiceover] Intel.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.450
- [Presenter] But then came AMD.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:31.050
In 2022, the longtime
rival took Intel's top spot

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.669
in market value, breaching a
market cap of over 200 billion.

00:00:35.669 --> 00:00:37.680
- It was the first time
that ever happened.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.360
- [Presenter] In part,
this recent achievement

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.430
can be traced back to one risky

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.000
and expensive decision AMD
made that changed the game

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.240
not just for the company,
but for computing.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.363
So how did AMD finally catch up to Intel?

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.130
July, 1968, Silicon Valley.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.620
A small group of engineers founded Intel,

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:00.510
a startup dedicated to chips,

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:03.390
which are essentially
the brains of a computer.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.060
Intel prioritized
development from the start

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:09.240
and didn't report any sales
in the first six months.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.750
Meanwhile, 20 minutes down
the road and 10 months later,

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:16.020
a former coworker of those
engineers founded AMD

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.420
or Advanced Micro Devices.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.450
AMD was making chips that
Intel had already made,

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.990
focusing more on keeping
up than getting ahead.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.900
Then in 1970, Intel came out with this,

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.420
the first semiconductor memory chip.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.570
A year later, it was the best
selling semiconductor device

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:34.620
in the world.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.320
AMD's hardware didn't sell the same way.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.410
- AMD really came up as
kind of an Intel copycat,

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.660
company that was making
alternatives to Intel chips

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.580
so like maybe a little bit
cheaper and things like that.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.120
So they kind of came in second
in a lot of ways to Intel,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:52.113
especially when it came to
CPUs and the rise of CPUs.

00:01:52.113 --> 00:01:54.360
- [Presenter] CPUs are
central processing units,

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.030
basically chips that solve problems.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.350
Here's how they work.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.110
First, the base, a flat piece of silicon.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.650
Silicon is a semiconductor,
which means it's conductive

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:08.070
but can be made more conductive
by adding other materials

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.380
like phosphorus or boron.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.530
On that silicon piece, there's
a pathway of transistors

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:16.140
which turn currents on and off.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:19.800
These strings of electronic
blips read as one or zero

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.830
and come together as binary information.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:26.070
That information goes
through this, a core.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.920
In simplified terms, the
core takes in a problem

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.450
and shoots out an answer.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.720
Those answers help determine
how to respond to requests

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.930
like opening a webpage
or sending an email.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.730
If the chip is the main brain,

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.910
the core is like a tiny second brain.

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.700
By 1971, Intel released the first

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:48.090
programmable microprocessor,
another success.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:51.000
The company churned out
new chip after new chip,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.570
creating industry standard
after industry standard.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:56.970
- Back in those times, it
was able to put together

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.550
this combination of marketing itself well

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.860
and creating some of the
world's best technology

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.160
to become the powerhouse
that it became back then.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:08.340
- [Presenter] By 1997, Intel
chips served as the brains

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:11.130
of 84% of the world's computers.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.110
It had 10 times AMD sales,

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.720
and six times its manufacturing capacity.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:16.770
- [Voiceover] Now anyone can have

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.150
all the brain power they want.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.910
Just look for a PC with
an Intel processor inside.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:22.350
- [Presenter] Going
into the new millennium,

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:26.130
AMD's market value lagged behind Intel's.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:30.090
Fast forward to late 2014,
when the company tasked new CEO

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.820
Lisa Su with turning things around.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.520
Before Su's tenure, the
company had fought off rumors

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.520
it would be sold and laid off workers.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.540
So behind the scenes, Su began
working on a new innovation.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.310
- I hope you guys have your money ready.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.380
We've made plenty of processors.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.560
- AMD released a new design for
their high performance chips

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.110
that beat many of Intel's benchmarks.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:55.170
First, the transistors
were organized in a new way

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:58.290
on the chips called Zen Architecture.

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.240
At the same time, AMD rethought

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.430
how chips themselves were made.

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.400
Intel had been focusing
on making a single CPU

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.100
answer questions really, really fast.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.530
AMD decided that for some of its chips,

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.420
it moved to chiplets.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.580
Instead of having one big silicon chip,

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.370
there were multiple small
chips in the same package,

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:20.910
connected together and
acting like one CPU.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.520
- That strategy conferred
some key advantages

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:26.340
in terms of power
consumption, in terms of cost,

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.520
but still performed very well
against Intel's products.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.190
- This chip redesign
was the pivotal factor

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:34.050
that gave AMD an edge.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:36.210
- And that really helped AMD return

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.640
to more of a leadership
position in the market

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.920
when these products launched in 2017

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:43.590
- Experts said that comparable AMD CPUs

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:46.620
were less expensive and
faster than Intel's.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.661
- Our competition is priced
at a thousand dollars.

00:04:49.661 --> 00:04:53.040
AMD Ryzen 499!

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.526
- By mid 2018, its market cap had shot up

00:04:56.526 --> 00:04:58.170
to over 30 billion dollars.

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.240
Almost exactly five
years after the release

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.050
of Ryzen in 2022, AMD
passed Intel in market cap

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:07.800
for the first time, but it
didn't hold that spot for long.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:08.700
Still...

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:10.890
- It reflects the investor sentiment

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:12.480
about the future of the two companies.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.810
Clearly, investors see
a lot of positive things

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:19.350
in the future for AMD, given
its share gains against Intel

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:21.180
- [Presenter] Then the sales of PCs,

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.270
which drive CPU usage, slumped.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.580
Shipments in the second quarter of 2022

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:30.120
dropped in the steepest
decline in nine years.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:32.790
In July, Intel posted
its biggest revenue drop

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:34.290
in more than a decade.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:38.250
A month later, AMD surpassed
Intel again in market value.

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:40.440
- Intel's trying to aggressively correct

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.320
its technology path and become, you know,

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:46.680
the undisputed leader again
in chip manufacturing.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.200
- [Presenter] While AMD was
also hit by market forces,

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:52.080
its revenue increased by 14%.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.230
- AMD is a little bit
more flexible in a sense

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:56.700
than Intel because it's smaller

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:59.370
and AMD does not have any
manufacturing facilities

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:00.300
like Intel does.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:03.090
That kind of gives AMD
some flexibility to adapt

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:06.780
to this environment, perhaps
without the kinds of cuts

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:08.430
that Intel is making.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:11.280
- But AMD may face even more challenges

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.980
after the US government
imposed export restrictions

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:16.440
on some chips in manufacturing equipment.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.300
CEO Lisa Sue said-

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.470
- [Su] It is minimal impact on
our revenue in the near term.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:25.500
- [Presenter] Intel is building
new factories called fabs

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:29.670
to help keep as much of
production as possible in the US.

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:32.490
- Where the oil reserves
are defined geopolitics

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:34.080
for the last five decades,

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.300
where the fabs are for
the next five decades

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:37.380
is more important.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.420
- [Presenter] Whether
AMD will continue to hold

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.520
its own against Intel will depend on

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.470
how well its innovations have laid

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:46.027
the groundwork for the way forward.

00:06:46.027 --> 00:06:48.944
(mysterious music)

